-- ---------- Attention test ----------
-- Two windows, two app ids: the offer (a small dialog) and the test surface.
-- Both must float. The test window is sized by the client to the exact width of
-- its 40-column grid, and tiling it would either squeeze that into a horizontal
-- scroll -- fatal, because the stop click has to land on a cell that was on
-- screen -- or shove the rest of the workspace aside for four minutes.
--
-- No `size` here on purpose: the width follows the cell size, which lives in
-- the test's own settings. One source of truth for how wide the grid is.
o.window("^dev\\.mordrud\\.attention$", { float = true, center = true })
o.window("^dev\\.mordrud\\.attention\\.prompt$", { float = true, center = true })

-- ---------- Vigilance test (PVT) ----------
-- Fixed-size and centred. The stimulus is a counter inside a box that sits in
-- one place for the whole run: a tiled window would put that box wherever the
-- layout happened to leave room, and the eye's starting position is part of the
-- reaction time being measured.
o.window("^dev\\.mordrud\\.pvt$", { float = true, center = true })
o.window("^dev\\.mordrud\\.pvt\\.prompt$", { float = true, center = true })

-- ---------- End of the working day ----------
-- A two-button question, put past half the norm when the clock has been stopped
-- for a while. Floating for the same reason as the other prompts: it is a
-- question, not a workspace, and it disappears the moment it is answered.
o.window("^dev\\.mordrud\\.study\\.sessionend$", { float = true, center = true })
